    "created_at": "2025-04-09T09:46:21Z",
    "updated_at": "2025-04-09T11:16:40Z",
    "body": "It seems there's two ways we could go about this:\n\n1. We use the existing \"redirect count\" primitive of requests, but make HTML also check and increment it for certain cases. Since HTML creates new requests in those cases you probably have to copy the value over to the new request. And it should probably all be limited to when we're doing an HTTPS upgrade.\n2. We introduce a new primitive solely for HTTPS upgrades. In this case we could either do loop detection or a limit. I think going with a limit again is more consistent, but perhaps there's rationale for not doing that?",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
